{
 "about": "Mercenary companies (decision 'Next 8': named companies; your follow-up answers: history's companies in their eras plus generated ones, hired at their hiring grounds, monthly pay plus a bonus after victories, 500 to 5,000 men of mixed arms, captains who can rise, going to whoever pays best). grounds: the hiring grounds where companies wait for employers (Cape Taenarum was the great one of the 4th and 3rd centuries BC; Crete and Rhodes sent archers and slingers; the Balearics slingers; the Celtic lands war bands). companies: from/to (years, negative BC) when the company can be hired; ground; units (unit id: count of units); captain (name, skills tactics/leadership/siegecraft/logistics/seamanship, perks); pay: [least, most] times what the same troops would cost as your own (pay and rations); an elite company's pay is drawn anew each year inside its range, so a strong company can bankrupt you; bonus: months of pay owed after each victory. Sources: Polybius 1.65-88 (the Mercenary War), 1.32-34 (Xanthippus), 2.21-31 (the Gaesatae at Telamon); Diodorus 21.18 and Polybius 1.7-8 (the Mamertines); Livy 38.16 and Pausanias 10.23 (the Galatians' crossing); Griffith, 'The Mercenaries of the Hellenistic World' (1935). Our own data, free for any use. men (unit id: men, added 18 Oct 2026; your answer 'Company sizes': to history's numbers, 500 to 5,000; each company is split into an even number of units): Taenarum's bands of a few thousand (Diodorus 17.111, 18.9); Xanthippus came with a small draft of Greek mercenaries (Polybius 1.32); Cretans 1,000 to 3,000 (Polybius 5.65, Raphia); Rhodian slingers at the 500 floor (Xenophon had 200, Anabasis 3.3); Leonnorios' half of the 10,000 Galatian fighters (Livy 38.16); the Gaesatae, a few thousand of the great Gallic host at Telamon (Polybius 2.23); the Mamertines, of whom Hiero defeated 8,000 (Polybius 1.9); Scopas' 6,000 Aetolians (Livy 31.43); the rest rounded to the bands' usual size in Griffith (1935). When 'men' is given it sets the company's size; 'units' still names the kinds of troops.",
